1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,506
A customer at the Corner Cafe asks Asha:
how much is the cake?

2
00:00:04,526 --> 00:00:07,127
Her menu is two lists from Chapter 11.

3
00:00:07,147 --> 00:00:11,610
One holds the names, and one holds the
prices, in the same order.

4
00:00:11,633 --> 00:00:17,014
To find the price, she must first find
where cake is, then read the same position

5
00:00:17,034 --> 00:00:18,281
in the second list.

6
00:00:18,301 --> 00:00:21,081
But customers never ask for item number 2.

7
00:00:21,101 --> 00:00:22,214
They ask by name.

8
00:00:22,820 --> 00:00:25,015
This is Code Quest, Chapter 12.

9
00:00:25,035 --> 00:00:27,087
Today you learn dictionaries.

10
00:00:27,107 --> 00:00:29,230
They find a value by its name.

11
00:00:29,267 --> 00:00:33,257
And an AI assistant will promise one
thing, and print another.

12
00:00:33,277 --> 00:00:34,422
You will catch it.

13
00:00:35,020 --> 00:00:36,973
So here is today's mission.

14
00:00:36,993 --> 00:00:42,380
Line 2 holds the whole menu in one
dictionary: every price under its name.

15
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:47,803
The customer types each item by name, and
the program counts the order in a second

16
00:00:47,823 --> 00:00:48,530
dictionary.

17
00:00:48,567 --> 00:00:51,150
Then it prints a receipt with the total.

18
00:00:51,170 --> 00:00:52,126
Let's build it.

19
00:00:52,717 --> 00:00:57,497
Read any value by its key, and add, change
or remove items.

20
00:00:57,533 --> 00:01:01,910
And choose between a list and a
dictionary, even when an AI chooses

21
00:01:01,930 --> 00:01:02,697
differently.

22
00:01:03,283 --> 00:01:07,065
A dictionary stores each value under a
name, called a key.

23
00:01:07,085 --> 00:01:10,473
You ask for the key, and Python gives you
the value.

24
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:13,782
Your phone finds a number by a contact's
name.

25
00:01:13,802 --> 00:01:16,294
A shop finds a product by its code.

26
00:01:16,314 --> 00:01:18,950
A game finds your score by your name.

27
00:01:19,550 --> 00:01:22,693
A dictionary is written with curly
brackets.

28
00:01:22,713 --> 00:01:25,641
Each item is a key, a colon, and a value.

29
00:01:25,661 --> 00:01:27,510
Commas separate the items.

30
00:01:27,533 --> 00:01:32,610
Here, the key tea points to 3.5, and the
key cake points to 4.25.

31
00:01:32,630 --> 00:01:35,903
Menu, cake in square brackets, gives 4.25.

32
00:01:35,933 --> 00:01:40,034
A value can be any type, even another
dictionary.

33
00:01:40,054 --> 00:01:44,323
Python keeps the items in the order you
added them.

34
00:01:44,367 --> 00:01:46,489
And each key appears only once.

35
00:01:46,509 --> 00:01:51,741
Like a name on the price board, but
stricter: add a key that already exists,

36
00:01:51,761 --> 00:01:54,437
and the new value replaces the old one.

37
00:01:55,053 --> 00:01:55,825
Quick check.

38
00:01:55,845 --> 00:01:58,596
In this dictionary, what is the word cake?

39
00:02:09,947 --> 00:02:10,105
C.

40
00:02:10,125 --> 00:02:10,642
A key.

41
00:02:10,662 --> 00:02:16,007
The part before the colon is the key, and
4.25 is its value.

42
00:02:16,620 --> 00:02:17,713
Take a breather.

43
00:02:17,733 --> 00:02:19,730
Here is what you know so far.

44
00:02:19,767 --> 00:02:22,825
Next, Asha writes the menu as a
dictionary.

45
00:02:27,550 --> 00:02:28,041
Step 1.

46
00:02:28,061 --> 00:02:33,300
Asha writes part of the menu: tea, coffee
and cake, each with its price.

47
00:02:33,333 --> 00:02:35,923
Line 3 prints the whole dictionary.

48
00:02:35,967 --> 00:02:38,414
Lines 4 and 5 read one value each.

49
00:02:38,434 --> 00:02:42,697
Square brackets again, but with a key
inside, not an index.

50
00:02:42,733 --> 00:02:44,137
And line 6 uses len.

51
00:02:44,157 --> 00:02:47,839
For a dictionary, it counts the key and
value pairs.

52
00:02:59,213 --> 00:03:04,203
3.5, the price of tea, and 4.25, the price
of cake.

53
00:03:04,233 --> 00:03:07,907
And len gives 3: three keys, each with its
value.

54
00:03:08,483 --> 00:03:08,954
Step 2.

55
00:03:08,974 --> 00:03:11,547
A dictionary can change, like a list.

56
00:03:11,567 --> 00:03:16,733
A key in square brackets, with an equals
sign and a value, adds a new key.

57
00:03:16,767 --> 00:03:20,867
The same line changes the value when the
key already exists.

58
00:03:20,900 --> 00:03:23,970
The word del removes a key and its value.

59
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:27,465
And pop removes a key, and gives its value
back to you.

60
00:03:28,087 --> 00:03:30,689
Line 3 adds a scone at 2.75.

61
00:03:30,709 --> 00:03:35,467
Scone is a new key, so the menu gets a
fourth item.

62
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:42,011
Line 5 uses the same kind of line, with
the key tea and the value 3.75.

63
00:03:53,380 --> 00:03:54,841
It changes the price.

64
00:03:54,861 --> 00:03:58,580
Tea is already a key, and each key appears
only once.

65
00:03:58,600 --> 00:04:01,010
Line 7 deletes coffee.

66
00:04:01,033 --> 00:04:02,660
And line 9 pops cake.

67
00:04:02,680 --> 00:04:08,931
The value, 4.25, goes into price, and line
10 prints it before the smaller menu.

68
00:04:09,517 --> 00:04:09,973
Step 3.

69
00:04:09,993 --> 00:04:13,920
If you ask for a key that is not there,
the program stops.

70
00:04:13,940 --> 00:04:16,847
So Python gives you two safe ways to
check.

71
00:04:16,867 --> 00:04:20,289
The word in checks whether a key is in the
dictionary.

72
00:04:20,309 --> 00:04:22,967
It checks the keys only, never the values.

73
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:25,786
And get gives the value if the key exists.

74
00:04:25,806 --> 00:04:30,328
If it does not, get gives None, or the
default value you put second.

75
00:04:41,680 --> 00:04:42,145
False.

76
00:04:42,165 --> 00:04:46,590
3.5 is a value, not a key, and in checks
the keys only.

77
00:04:46,633 --> 00:04:49,142
Line 6 gets the price of cake.

78
00:04:49,162 --> 00:04:52,177
Line 7 asks for juice and gets None.

79
00:04:52,197 --> 00:04:55,296
Line 8 gives a default, so it gets 0.

80
00:04:55,883 --> 00:04:56,324
Step 4.

81
00:04:56,344 --> 00:05:00,670
A for loop over a dictionary goes through
its keys, one at a time.

82
00:05:00,690 --> 00:05:02,843
So lines 3 and 4 print the names.

83
00:05:02,867 --> 00:05:06,484
To get each key together with its value,
use items.

84
00:05:06,504 --> 00:05:09,907
Line 5 takes two loop variables, name and
price.

85
00:05:09,933 --> 00:05:13,581
Keys and values give the keys or the
values on their own.

86
00:05:13,601 --> 00:05:16,733
List turns them into lists so they print
clearly.

87
00:05:16,767 --> 00:05:18,628
And sum adds the values.

88
00:05:29,980 --> 00:05:36,671
Each name with its price, lined up with
the codes from Chapter 3: tea 3.50, coffee

89
00:05:36,691 --> 00:05:37,980
4.00, cake 4.25.

90
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:41,674
And lines 3 and 4 printed the names only.

91
00:05:41,694 --> 00:05:45,278
A loop over a dictionary gives its keys.

92
00:05:45,298 --> 00:05:46,900
Remember this one.

93
00:05:46,933 --> 00:05:51,733
Then the keys, the values, and their sum:
11.75.

94
00:05:52,317 --> 00:05:52,832
Step 5.

95
00:05:52,852 --> 00:05:57,116
Asha wants to know how many of each item
she sold today.

96
00:05:57,136 --> 00:05:58,876
Line 2 lists the sales.

97
00:05:58,896 --> 00:06:03,007
Line 3 starts an empty dictionary: two
curly brackets.

98
00:06:03,033 --> 00:06:04,921
Line 5 does the counting.

99
00:06:04,941 --> 00:06:09,803
Get gives the count so far, or 0 the first
time an item appears.

100
00:06:09,833 --> 00:06:15,253
Python works out the right side first,
adds 1, and stores the new count under the

101
00:06:15,273 --> 00:06:16,058
item's name.

102
00:06:27,413 --> 00:06:29,278
Tea 3, cake 2, scone 1.

103
00:06:29,298 --> 00:06:32,473
Each name appears once, with its count.

104
00:06:32,500 --> 00:06:36,488
And line 7 reads one count by its name: 3
teas.

105
00:06:37,087 --> 00:06:37,584
Step 6.

106
00:06:37,604 --> 00:06:40,096
A value can be another dictionary.

107
00:06:40,116 --> 00:06:43,422
Here, each item has a price and a vegan
flag.

108
00:06:43,442 --> 00:06:45,417
This is called nested data.

109
00:06:45,467 --> 00:06:50,608
Line 7 reads it in two steps: menu cake
gives the inner dictionary, and price

110
00:06:50,628 --> 00:06:52,217
reads one value from it.

111
00:06:52,267 --> 00:06:54,824
The loop prints only the vegan items.

112
00:06:54,844 --> 00:07:00,397
Inside the f-string on line 10, the key
uses single quotation marks, because the

113
00:07:00,417 --> 00:07:02,209
f-string uses double ones.

114
00:07:02,820 --> 00:07:03,307
Breather.

115
00:07:03,327 --> 00:07:05,563
You now have every piece of the mission.

116
00:07:05,583 --> 00:07:06,916
Let's put them together.

117
00:07:11,650 --> 00:07:13,183
Back to the mission.

118
00:07:13,203 --> 00:07:14,658
Line 2 is the menu.

119
00:07:14,678 --> 00:07:16,910
Line 3 starts an empty order.

120
00:07:16,933 --> 00:07:20,925
The loop asks for items until the customer
types done.

121
00:07:20,945 --> 00:07:26,273
Line 5 removes spaces and capital letters,
so Tea and tea both find tea.

122
00:07:26,300 --> 00:07:29,994
Line 8 checks the menu before anything is
looked up.

123
00:07:30,014 --> 00:07:32,423
A known item is counted on line 9.

124
00:07:32,443 --> 00:07:35,780
Anything else gets a polite message on
line 11.

125
00:07:35,800 --> 00:07:41,924
Then lines 12 to 17 go through the order
with items, work out each cost, add up the

126
00:07:41,944 --> 00:07:43,997
total and print the receipt.

127
00:07:55,380 --> 00:07:55,656
Two.

128
00:07:55,676 --> 00:08:01,290
Capital Tea and tea with a space both
become tea, so line 9 counts it twice.

129
00:08:01,333 --> 00:08:03,498
Juice is not on the menu.

130
00:08:03,518 --> 00:08:07,953
Line 11 prints a polite message, and
nothing stops.

131
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:09,371
The total is 11.25.

132
00:08:09,391 --> 00:08:10,616
Mission complete!

133
00:08:10,636 --> 00:08:16,253
Next, Asha tracks the cafe's stock with an
AI assistant, one stage at a time.

134
00:08:16,850 --> 00:08:20,337
This chapter's AI skill is challenging a
design.

135
00:08:20,357 --> 00:08:23,186
Ask AI how to store your data, and why.

136
00:08:23,206 --> 00:08:27,935
Then compare its answer with the job, and
keep the safety habits.

137
00:08:28,553 --> 00:08:29,653
Stage 1: plan.

138
00:08:29,673 --> 00:08:34,533
Asha describes her stock: 6 cakes, 4
scones and 5 sandwiches.

139
00:08:34,553 --> 00:08:39,173
She finds items by name, and takes one
away when it sells.

140
00:08:39,200 --> 00:08:43,775
The AI suggests two lists: one for the
names, one for the counts.

141
00:08:43,795 --> 00:08:46,602
To sell a cake, find its position first.

142
00:08:47,220 --> 00:08:50,511
That is the problem from the start of this
video.

143
00:08:50,531 --> 00:08:54,161
So Asha challenges it: I always look items
up by name.

144
00:08:54,181 --> 00:08:55,580
Why not a dictionary?

145
00:08:55,600 --> 00:08:56,683
The AI agrees.

146
00:08:56,703 --> 00:09:00,384
A dictionary finds the count directly, by
name.

147
00:09:00,987 --> 00:09:02,057
Stage 2: write.

148
00:09:02,077 --> 00:09:08,087
Asha writes the first lines herself, and
asks the AI to explain line 3 with her own

149
00:09:08,107 --> 00:09:08,887
dictionary.

150
00:09:08,933 --> 00:09:16,213
The AI explains: the right side first, 6
minus 1 is 5, then 5 is stored under cake.

151
00:09:16,233 --> 00:09:17,080
She runs it.

152
00:09:17,100 --> 00:09:19,608
The worked example matches the run.

153
00:09:20,220 --> 00:09:21,476
Stage 3: generate.

154
00:09:21,496 --> 00:09:24,523
Asha sold a cake, a scone and another
cake.

155
00:09:24,543 --> 00:09:30,050
She asks for code that prints how many of
each are left, and the exact output.

156
00:09:30,100 --> 00:09:33,976
The AI promises: Left 4, Left 3, Left 5.

157
00:09:33,996 --> 00:09:35,437
Look at line 6.

158
00:09:46,813 --> 00:09:47,007
No.

159
00:09:47,027 --> 00:09:51,064
Remember step 4: a loop over a dictionary
gives its keys.

160
00:09:51,084 --> 00:09:54,836
The variable is called count, but it holds
the names.

161
00:09:55,453 --> 00:09:56,473
Asha runs it.

162
00:09:56,493 --> 00:09:57,274
Left cake.

163
00:09:57,294 --> 00:09:58,154
Left scone.

164
00:09:58,174 --> 00:09:59,274
Left sandwich.

165
00:09:59,294 --> 00:10:04,154
A name for a variable does not change what
Python puts in it.

166
00:10:04,753 --> 00:10:05,762
Stage 4: test.

167
00:10:05,782 --> 00:10:10,463
Asha asks for a fix, and three test cases
with expected outputs.

168
00:10:10,483 --> 00:10:16,413
Each case starts with 2 cakes, and one
sells a muffin, which is not in her stock.

169
00:10:16,433 --> 00:10:17,803
The fix uses items.

170
00:10:17,823 --> 00:10:22,116
And for the muffin, the AI predicts: the
muffin is ignored.

171
00:10:33,480 --> 00:10:34,730
The program stops.

172
00:10:34,750 --> 00:10:39,668
Remember step 3: asking for a key that is
not there stops the program.

173
00:10:40,287 --> 00:10:44,199
First, Asha runs the AI's fix on today's
sales.

174
00:10:44,219 --> 00:10:46,541
Cake 4, scone 3, sandwich 5.

175
00:10:46,561 --> 00:10:49,888
Now each line shows a name and a number.

176
00:10:50,487 --> 00:10:51,734
Then the cases.

177
00:10:51,754 --> 00:10:54,523
For each one, she sets cake to 2.

178
00:10:54,543 --> 00:10:57,819
No sales: 2, 4 and 5 left, as expected.

179
00:10:57,839 --> 00:10:58,242
Pass.

180
00:10:58,853 --> 00:11:01,612
The last two cakes: cake, 0 left.

181
00:11:01,632 --> 00:11:02,033
Pass.

182
00:11:02,653 --> 00:11:07,018
Case 3, a cake and a muffin, stops with a
Key Error: muffin.

183
00:11:07,038 --> 00:11:09,211
Two cases pass, and one fails.

184
00:11:09,231 --> 00:11:13,449
The AI's prediction was wrong, and only
the run showed it.

185
00:11:14,053 --> 00:11:15,170
Stage 5: debug.

186
00:11:15,190 --> 00:11:20,473
Asha pastes the code and the full error,
and asks for the cause first.

187
00:11:20,500 --> 00:11:24,591
The AI names line 5: the key muffin is not
in the dictionary.

188
00:11:24,611 --> 00:11:29,780
The smallest fix: check with in first, and
print a message for anything else.

189
00:11:30,387 --> 00:11:32,955
A fix can break a case that worked before.

190
00:11:32,975 --> 00:11:36,036
So Asha runs all three cases again with
the check.

191
00:11:36,056 --> 00:11:40,597
Lines 2 to 6 keep the cases in a
dictionary: each name points to a list of

192
00:11:40,617 --> 00:11:40,967
sales.

193
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:45,734
Line 9 resets the stock to 2 cakes at the
start of each case.

194
00:11:45,754 --> 00:11:47,760
No sales: 2, 4 and 5 left.

195
00:11:47,780 --> 00:11:48,150
Pass.

196
00:11:48,200 --> 00:11:51,115
The last two cakes: cake, 0 left.

197
00:11:51,135 --> 00:11:51,560
Pass.

198
00:11:51,600 --> 00:11:55,418
And the muffin: a message, and the other
counts are right.

199
00:11:55,438 --> 00:11:55,749
Pass.

200
00:11:55,769 --> 00:11:57,139
All three cases pass.

201
00:11:57,753 --> 00:11:59,010
Stage 6: improve.

202
00:11:59,030 --> 00:12:02,989
Asha asks for one change that makes the
code shorter.

203
00:12:03,009 --> 00:12:06,893
And she adds: the output must stay exactly
the same.

204
00:12:06,933 --> 00:12:12,853
The AI suggests one line with get and a
default of 0, so the check is not needed.

205
00:12:12,873 --> 00:12:15,346
It says the output stays the same.

206
00:12:15,953 --> 00:12:18,496
Asha tests it with the muffin case.

207
00:12:18,516 --> 00:12:22,669
Line 5 gets the muffin's count, or 0, and
takes one away.

208
00:12:34,047 --> 00:12:34,274
No.

209
00:12:34,294 --> 00:12:40,617
Get gives 0 for the muffin, minus 1 makes
minus 1, and line 5 adds a new key.

210
00:12:40,637 --> 00:12:44,407
The output gains a line: muffin, minus 1
left.

211
00:12:44,433 --> 00:12:50,151
A stock of minus one muffin makes no
sense, and the AI ignored her instruction.

212
00:12:50,171 --> 00:12:51,676
Asha keeps her check.

213
00:12:52,287 --> 00:12:53,138
Quick check.

214
00:12:53,158 --> 00:12:58,581
An AI designs your data as two lists, but
you always look items up by name.

215
00:12:58,601 --> 00:12:59,670
What do you do?

216
00:13:11,047 --> 00:13:11,175
A.

217
00:13:11,195 --> 00:13:13,689
Ask why, and suggest a dictionary.

218
00:13:13,709 --> 00:13:17,386
Challenge every design against the job it
must do.

219
00:13:17,987 --> 00:13:18,590
Breather.

220
00:13:18,610 --> 00:13:24,338
Asha challenged a design, and caught three
AI mistakes: names instead of numbers, a

221
00:13:24,358 --> 00:13:28,424
crash it did not predict, and a change
that broke her rule.

222
00:13:28,444 --> 00:13:32,026
Next, the mistakes beginners make with
dictionaries.

223
00:13:36,750 --> 00:13:37,591
Mistake 1.

224
00:13:37,611 --> 00:13:40,260
Line 3 prints the price of tea.

225
00:13:40,300 --> 00:13:43,373
Line 4 asks for juice, which is not a key.

226
00:13:43,393 --> 00:13:47,570
Python stops with a Key Error, and names
the missing key.

227
00:13:47,600 --> 00:13:52,688
The fix: check with in first, or use get
with a default when a default makes sense.

228
00:13:53,287 --> 00:13:54,184
Now you try.

229
00:13:54,204 --> 00:13:58,697
Line 3 uses get to find the price of tea,
with a capital T.

230
00:13:58,717 --> 00:14:03,822
Keys are case-sensitive: capital T and
small t make different keys.

231
00:14:15,180 --> 00:14:15,524
None.

232
00:14:15,544 --> 00:14:18,724
Capital Tea is not a key, so get gives
None.

233
00:14:18,744 --> 00:14:21,560
Get did not stop the program on line 3.

234
00:14:21,600 --> 00:14:27,480
But line 5 tries to multiply None, and the
program stops there with a Type Error.

235
00:14:27,500 --> 00:14:32,292
The fix: use lower on names before you
look them up, and check what get gives

236
00:14:32,312 --> 00:14:32,542
you.

237
00:14:33,153 --> 00:14:33,925
One more.

238
00:14:33,945 --> 00:14:38,675
Line 2 writes tea twice: once at 3.5, and
once at 3.0.

239
00:14:38,695 --> 00:14:42,897
Remember the one-key rule from the idea
section.

240
00:14:54,280 --> 00:14:54,551
Two.

241
00:14:54,571 --> 00:15:00,370
Each key appears only once, so the second
tea replaced the first, with no error.

242
00:15:00,400 --> 00:15:04,147
The fix: check that every key in a
dictionary is different.

243
00:15:04,753 --> 00:15:08,011
Mistake 4 is the AI's mistake from stage
3.

244
00:15:08,031 --> 00:15:11,593
Line 4 loops over the menu, and expects
prices.

245
00:15:11,633 --> 00:15:14,330
But price holds the key tea, a string.

246
00:15:14,350 --> 00:15:19,333
Python cannot add a string to a number, so
it stops with a Type Error.

247
00:15:19,367 --> 00:15:24,714
The fix: loop over menu dot values, or use
items when you need both.

248
00:15:25,320 --> 00:15:26,163
Last breather.

249
00:15:26,183 --> 00:15:30,356
Now you know the four dictionary mistakes
that catch most beginners.

250
00:15:35,083 --> 00:15:35,471
First.

251
00:15:35,491 --> 00:15:37,783
Two tables order at the same time.

252
00:15:37,803 --> 00:15:43,223
How could a dictionary with table numbers
as keys, and lists of items as values,

253
00:15:43,243 --> 00:15:44,855
keep each table's order?

254
00:15:56,213 --> 00:16:00,033
Write one after the video, and print the
order for table 4.

255
00:16:00,653 --> 00:16:01,094
Second.

256
00:16:01,114 --> 00:16:03,330
A customer types cakes, with an s.

257
00:16:03,350 --> 00:16:06,552
How could the order taker also accept the
plural?

258
00:16:06,572 --> 00:16:07,801
And is it worth it?

259
00:16:19,180 --> 00:16:22,295
Try one idea, and test it with three
answers.

260
00:16:22,883 --> 00:16:23,631
Let's recap.

261
00:16:23,651 --> 00:16:26,060
A dictionary stores values under keys.

262
00:16:26,080 --> 00:16:28,553
Ask for the key, and you get the value.

263
00:16:28,600 --> 00:16:32,260
The same line adds a new key or changes an
existing one.

264
00:16:32,300 --> 00:16:33,514
In checks the keys.

265
00:16:33,534 --> 00:16:36,500
Get gives the value, or None, or your
default.

266
00:16:36,533 --> 00:16:39,103
A loop over a dictionary gives its keys.

267
00:16:39,123 --> 00:16:41,433
Items gives each key with its value.

268
00:16:41,467 --> 00:16:45,077
Get, with 0, plus 1, counts things by
name.

269
00:16:45,100 --> 00:16:49,244
And with AI: ask for a design, then
challenge it against the job.

270
00:16:49,853 --> 00:16:50,900
One last check.

271
00:16:50,920 --> 00:16:52,251
Counts holds tea 2.

272
00:16:52,271 --> 00:16:54,882
Then the counting line runs for cake.

273
00:16:54,902 --> 00:16:56,233
What is counts now?

274
00:17:07,613 --> 00:17:07,765
B.

275
00:17:07,785 --> 00:17:09,315
Tea 2, and cake 1.

276
00:17:09,335 --> 00:17:14,910
Get gave 0 for the new key, plus 1 is 1,
and the line added cake.

277
00:17:15,520 --> 00:17:16,851
Mission complete!

278
00:17:16,871 --> 00:17:20,267
You earned 100 XP and the Key Master
badge.

279
00:17:22,517 --> 00:17:24,088
Now it is your turn.

280
00:17:24,108 --> 00:17:27,907
The practice file has eight tasks, worth
170 XP.

281
00:17:27,933 --> 00:17:31,223
Three warm-up tasks, worth 10 XP each.

282
00:17:31,267 --> 00:17:35,117
Three apply tasks, worth 20 each,
including counting votes.

283
00:17:35,167 --> 00:17:40,851
And two stretch tasks, worth 40 each: an
order with quantities, and an AI's order

284
00:17:40,871 --> 00:17:41,767
total to fix.

285
00:17:41,800 --> 00:17:43,442
Predict first, then run.

286
00:17:43,462 --> 00:17:46,421
Try each task before you open the
solution.

287
00:17:47,017 --> 00:17:50,749
Asha's list of today's customers has Omar
in it four times.

288
00:17:50,769 --> 00:17:53,103
How many different customers came in?

289
00:17:53,123 --> 00:17:54,757
A list keeps every repeat.

290
00:17:54,800 --> 00:18:00,490
In Chapter 13, you will meet tuples, fixed
groups of values, and sets, which keep

291
00:18:00,510 --> 00:18:01,900
each item only once.

292
00:18:01,933 --> 00:18:03,744
Do the warm-up tasks now.

293
00:18:03,764 --> 00:18:05,793
They take about ten minutes.

294
00:18:05,833 --> 00:18:07,810
See you in Chapter 13.
